        "section": "pathology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Environmental, Nutritional, and Toxic Injury",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The novice mental model is \"toxins are poisonous; deficiencies cause weakness.\" The expert model\nis a **dose-at-target problem**: an external agent must be *delivered* to a tissue, reach an\neffective *dose at its molecular target*, and act by a *mechanism*, before any lesion appears —\nand the same agent can be harmless, therapeutic, or lethal depending on dose, route, duration,\nand host. The organizing principle, attributed to Paracelsus (16th century) — *\"the dose makes\nthe poison\"* — applies to **everything** in this guide: physical energy, chemicals, and even\nnutrients, each of which injures by **deficiency, sufficiency, or excess**."
    },
